                    "messages": [
                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-0",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "I'll write the optimized `run.py` directly, replacing the two-kernel mul+sum with a single fused cuBLAS batched dot-product launch — a different direction from round 1's copy elimination.",
                        "time": "刚刚"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-1",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round 2 replaces the two-kernel `mul` + `sum(dim=-1)` sequence with a single fused cuBLAS batched dot-product: `torch.bmm(x.unsqueeze(1), y.unsqueeze(2)).view(-1)`. This is a different direction from the rejected candidate-01 digest (which only dropped the trailing `.clone()`); the new diff attacks kernel-launch count rather than a redundant copy. Round-1 measurements (baseline 11.264us with 3 launches -> candidate-01 8.192us with 2 launches) imply ~3us per launch on this runner plus ~2.2us fixed per-call overhead, so a 1-launch formulation is the only remaining bounded win at 32x256. TF32 is explicitly disabled at import so the cuBLAS path keeps fp32 accumulate numerics matching the oracle within atol/rtol=1e-5. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Latency at shape 32x256 is launch-bound, not bandwidth- or FLOP-bound: the tensor has only 8192 fp32 elements, so the arithmetic costs less than the per-kernel dispatch. Round-1 evidence quantifies this: removing one kernel launch (the redundant clone) moved p50 from 11.264us to 8.192us, i.e. ~3.07us per launch, leaving ~2.1-2.2us of fixed per-call Python/event overhead. The remaining reducible cost is the second launch (the standalone last-dim reduction kernel).\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"Two CUDA kernel launches per call: elementwise mul kernel, then a separate last-dim reduction kernel (~3us each at this size).\",\"Fixed per-call host overhead (~2.2us) from dict lookups and tensor dispatch, which cannot be removed without caching results (semantically unsafe).\"]},\"patch\":\"\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"Fuse elementwise multiply and last-dim reduction into one cuBLAS kernel\",\"hypothesis\":\"At 32x256 the operator is launch-latency bound (~3us per kernel launch, measured from the 11.264us -> 8.192us delta when candidate-01 removed one launch). Rewriting the row-wise sum of products as a batched (rows,1,cols)@(rows,cols,1) matrix product collapses mul+sum into a single cuBLAS launch, so p50 should drop to roughly one launch plus fixed overhead, i.e. ~5us, instead of the current two launches at 8.192us.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now returns torch.bmm(inputs['x'].unsqueeze(1), inputs['y'].unsqueeze(2)).view(-1). The unsqueezes and view(-1) are metadata-only (no device kernels), so exactly one kernel launch remains. torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 is set to False at import (guarded by try/except) so cuBLAS cannot silently select TF32 tensor-core kernels, which would break the atol/rtol=1e-5 comparison against the fp32-accumulating reference. get_inputs, get_test_cases, get_benchmark_inputs and reference are unchanged from the baseline, and shapes are preserved: (rows,1,1).view(-1) -> (rows,) matches reference(inputs) for every case (1x1, 32x256, 2x32, 3x17).\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[\"baseline run.py (naive_v0, 11.264000087976456us, local-shared-gpu)\",\"round-1 candidate-01 evidence: 8.191999979317188us, correctness 4/4, gate performance.target failed\"],\"risks\":[\"Accumulation order inside cuBLAS differs from torch.sum's tree reduction, so diffs versus the oracle are no longer exactly 0. Magnitude is bounded well inside tolerance for these inputs (all products positive, |ref| ~ 2645 for the primary case, giving tol ~2.6e-2 versus an expected order-of-magnitude error of ~1e-3), but it is not bit-identical as candidate-01 was.\",\"If the runner environment forces TF32 on despite the import-time flag, precision would degrade far beyond tolerance; the flag is set defensively and wrapped in try/except for API-compatibility across torch versions.\",\"Global torch backend state is set once at import; this touches matmul precision only and does not affect the reference path, which uses mul/sum.\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02 for shared-GPU correctness + benchmark\",\"reason\":\"The edit is a single bounded change to the run(inputs) implementation path in run.py, with all contract functions preserved. It targets the dominant remaining cost (kernel launch count) identified from round-1 measurements, and is a different direction from the rejected candidate-01 digest. Local test execution is prohibited in this stage, and Operator Studio owns the serialized verification queue.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness across minimal/representative/boundary/ragged on local-shared-gpu, plus a primary-profile p50 for candidate-02 to compare against the 8.192us round-1 candidate and the 11.264us baseline.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Non-zero (though small) numerical diff versus the oracle replaces candidate-01's exact-match behavior; correctness gate will confirm it stays within atol/rtol=1e-5.\",\"The 99.9999% improvement target implies a required latency of ~1e-5 us, which is physically unreachable on this runner; this candidate narrows the achievable gap rather than meeting that threshold.\",\"cuBLAS kernel-selection heuristics for m=n=1 batched GEMM are not observable without running on the GPU; a poor tile choice could offset some of the launch-count saving.\"]}",
                        "time": "刚刚"
                      }
