                    {
                      "id": "candidate-02",
                      "version": "agent.2",
                      "label": "Single-reduction normalization via vector_norm + fused eps/scale addcmul",
                      "title": "Single-reduction normalization via vector_norm + fused eps/scale addcmul",
                      "hypothesis": "If per-call latency is dominated by ~3us kernel launches rather than by memory traffic, replacing (x*x, mean, add, rsqrt, mul) with (vector_norm, addcmul, rsqrt_, mul) removes one launch and two full-size tensor allocations, cutting p50 from 15.36us toward ~12us on the primary 32x256 profile.",
                      "change": "run(inputs): instead of `value = inputs['x'] * torch.rsqrt((inputs['x'] * inputs['x']).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-5); return value.clone()`, now compute `norm = torch.linalg.vector_norm(x, dim=-1, keepdim=True)` (sum(x*x) accumulated inside one reduction kernel), then `mean_eps = torch.addcmul(<cached eps tile>, norm, norm, value=1.0/cols)`, and return `x * mean_eps.rsqrt_()`. A read-only eps tile is cached per (shape, device, dtype) via `_eps_buffer` and is never mutated because addcmul is used out-of-place. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline.",
                      "files": "run.py",
                      "status": "弱候选参考",
