                        "id": "claude-event-0",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round 2 candidate: rewrote run(inputs) to derive the per-row statistic from a single L2-norm reduction instead of materializing x*x and then reducing it, and folded the 1/cols scale plus the eps offset into one addcmul. Kernel launches drop from 5 (candidate-01) / 6 (baseline) to 4, and the two full-size intermediates (x*x, and the separate normalization result) are gone. Correctness is preserved algebraically: out = x * rsqrt(eps + norm^2/cols) with 1/cols an exact power-of-two scale for the primary profile; run() never mutates inputs['x'], so repeated benchmark iterations and the reference oracle stay valid. Direction is deliberately different from candidate-01 (which only removed the trailing clone and made the eps add in-place) — this one attacks the reduction itself.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Measured evidence shows per-call cost is launch-bound, not bandwidth-bound: baseline 6 launches = 17.408us and candidate-01's 5 launches = 15.36us both imply ~2.9-3.1us per launch, while the actual data movement (32x256 fp32 = 32KB per tensor, ~96KB minimum traffic) is sub-microsecond on any sm86 part. The primary bottleneck is therefore the number of dependent kernel launches on the Windows/shared-GPU path, followed by per-op allocation count; arithmetic and memory traffic are not the limiter. torch.compile / CUDA-graph fusion was considered and rejected: per-call dynamo guard + wrapper overhead (~10-30us) is larger than the ~4 eager launches it would replace, and it adds compile-time and environment failure modes on a shared runner.\",\"bottlenecks\":[{\"name\":\"kernel_launch_count\",\"evidence\":\"baseline 6 launches -> 17.408us, candidate-01 5 launches -> 15.36us (~3us/launch, arithmetic identical in both)\",\"plan\":\"cut to 4 launches by using one norm reduction for sum(x*x) and one addcmul for scale+eps\"},{\"name\":\"redundant_full_size_intermediates\",\"evidence\":\"baseline materializes x*x (32KB write + 32KB read) and a separate cloned output\",\"plan\":\"norm reduction consumes x directly; no x*x tensor is ever written\"},{\"name\":\"small_tensor_kernel_count\",\"evidence\":\"eps add and rsqrt each cost a full launch despite operating on 32 elements\",\"plan\":\"addcmul folds the 1/cols scale and eps offset into a single tiny kernel, leaving rsqrt_ in place on its fresh output\"}]},\"patch\":\"\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"Single-reduction normalization via vector_norm + fused eps/scale addcmul\",\"hypothesis\":\"If per-call latency is dominated by ~3us kernel launches rather than by memory traffic, replacing (x*x, mean, add, rsqrt, mul) with (vector_norm, addcmul, rsqrt_, mul) removes one launch and two full-size tensor allocations, cutting p50 from 15.36us toward ~12us on the primary 32x256 profile.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs): instead of `value = inputs['x'] * torch.rsqrt((inputs['x'] * inputs['x']).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-5); return value.clone()`, now compute `norm = torch.linalg.vector_norm(x, dim=-1, keepdim=True)` (sum(x*x) accumulated inside one reduction kernel), then `mean_eps = torch.addcmul(<cached eps tile>, norm, norm, value=1.0/cols)`, and return `x * mean_eps.rsqrt_()`. A read-only eps tile is cached per (shape, device, dtype) via `_eps_buffer` and is never mutated because addcmul is used out-of-place. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[\"embedded baseline run.py (naive_v0, 17.408us @ local-shared-gpu)\",\"round-1 candidate-01 evidence: 5 launches, 15.359999611973763us, Accept Gate performance.target not met\"],\"risks\":[\"norm^2 introduces one extra fp32 sqrt/square round-trip vs summing x*x directly; expected relative deviation ~2e-7, far inside atol/rtol 1e-5, but it is the only numerical change in the candidate\",\"1/cols for the ragged profile (cols=17) is not a power of two, so mean is reproduced as multiply-by-reciprocal rather than divide-by-n; deviation is ~1 ulp\",\"if torch.linalg.vector_norm lowers to two kernels on this build the launch count is unchanged versus candidate-01, making the round a wash rather than a regression\",\"module-level tensor cache holds small (rows,1) CUDA buffers for the lifetime of the process\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02 for shared-GPU correctness + benchmark\",\"reason\":\"It is the only remaining bounded eager-PyTorch lever with real headroom: 4 launches vs 5/6 previously, with correctness preserved algebraically. The 99.9999% target is unreachable by construction (it implies ~1.7e-5us, below any single launch), so the achievable goal is monotone launch-count reduction backed by measured p50.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness on primary and small profiles, plus p50_us for the primary profile to compare against candidate-01 (15.36us) and baseline (17.408us).\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Performance gate is mathematically unreachable: 99.9999% of 17.408us is ~1.7e-5us, far below one kernel launch (~3us measured), so this round can improve p50 but cannot satisfy performance.target.\",\"Candidate-02 is unverified until the serialized GPU queue runs it; the 4-launch hypothesis rests on torch.linalg.vector_norm lowering to a single reduction kernel on this torch build.\",\"The cached eps tile keeps small CUDA allocations alive across the whole run; harmless for this harness but stateful.\"]}",
                        "time": "刚刚"
